				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${[\theta ]}_{{{\rm{MRW}}}}\left({{\rm{deg}}}\times {{{\rm{cm}}}}^{2}\times {{{\rm{dmol}}}}^{-1}\right)=\frac{\theta ({{\rm{mdeg}}})\times {{\rm{MRW}}}}{{{\rm{L}}}({{\rm{mm}}})\times {{\rm{c}}}({{\rm{mg}}}/{{\rm{mL}}})}$$\end{document}[θ]MRWdeg×cm2×dmol−1=θ(mdeg)×MRWL(mm)×c(mg/mL)where [θ]MRW is the mean molar ellipticity by residue, MRW is the molecular weight of peptides divided by the number of residues minus one, θ is the ellipticity, L is the optical path (1.0 mm) and, c is the concentration.

The conformational shifts (ΔδHα and ΔδCα) were obtained as the differences between the observed chemical shifts and those in random coil (RC) peptides: ΔδHα = δHαobserved – δHαRC, ppm and ΔδCα = δCαobserved – δCαRC, ppm δHαRC and δHαRC were taken from Wishart et al (Wishart et al, 1995). Helix populations (Appendix Table S10) were estimated from the 1Hα and 13Cα chemical shifts as previously described (Chaves-Arquero et al, 2020; Sandín et al, 2021). A weighted value for the chemical shift changes (Δδw, ppm) was defined as:\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
